WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:08.140
Proposition forty-five changes environmental review for eligible construction projects.

2
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:13.820
California’s environmental quality law requires agencies to examine significant

3
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.900
impacts before approving many projects.

4
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:22.480
Review can identify harms and alternatives, while delay can

5
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.420
also make projects more costly.

6
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.380
The proposal offers an optional faster process for specified

7
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:34.360
projects, including many housing, transportation, water, health, and clean-energy

8
00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:34.940
projects.

9
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.220
It changes deadlines and the scope of review and

10
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.280
litigation, not just the speed of paperwork.

11
00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.400
Imagine a planner preparing a new housing project.

12
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.820
Residents may want homes built sooner but also want

13
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:52.020
traffic, water, and environmental impacts examined.

14
00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:53.960
Those goals can overlap.

15
00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:57.840
The disagreement is whether the proposed process keeps enough

16
00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.740
scrutiny while reducing avoidable delay.

17
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.340
The surrounding community lives with the completed project.

18
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:08.220
For projects using the new route, agencies would set

19
00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.780
application requirements upfront and face tighter deadlines.

20
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:18.820
For example, missing a three-hundred-sixty-five-business-day environmental-report deadline could trigger

21
00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.760
a hearing request and potentially a lawsuit.

22
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.860
It is not automatic approval after a year.

23
00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:28.500
Review would use standards in place when the application

24
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:29.280
is submitted.

25
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.900
The applicant could offer one alternative instead of an

26
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.520
agency developing several.

27
00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:39.160
Public-comment periods would have maximums, and required tribal consultation

28
00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:40.740
would be narrower.

29
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:44.460
Courts would have less scope to review challenges and

30
00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:47.640
could stop the noncompliant portion rather than the whole

31
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.200
project.

32
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.320
Other permits and legal requirements still matter.

33
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:55.220
Faster review does not mean every project becomes buildable

34
00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:55.860
or affordable.

35
00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.500
Initial government costs could reach the high tens of

36
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:04.060
millions annually, potentially over one hundred million, partly covered

37
00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:04.680
by fees.

38
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:09.920
Longer-term effects are uncertain: faster construction may save money,

39
00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.120
while additional environmental harm could create costs.

40
00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:17.380
A voter might support more predictable schedules and fewer

41
00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:22.080
project-wide shutdowns, hoping to deliver homes and infrastructure sooner.

42
00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.100
They may judge delay itself costly.

43
00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.660
That benefit depends on actual projects moving forward, not

44
00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:31.560
merely shorter review documents.

45
00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.860
You might ask what evidence or voices could be

46
00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:35.260
missed.

47
00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.660
Another voter could object to fewer alternatives, tighter public

48
00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:43.160
participation, or narrower court and tribal review.

49
00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:47.080
They may worry that faster decisions shift costs onto

50
00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:48.500
communities or the environment.

51
00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.880
The question is the balance of speed and accountability,

52
00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.220
not whether review should exist at all.

53
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.480
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 49

54
00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:03.380
percent said yes, 46 percent no, and 5 percent

55
00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:03.920
were unsure.

56
00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.160
These are dated survey results, not votes already cast.

57
00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:13.420
Yes creates the optional new procedures; no keeps existing

58
00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:14.920
review and challenge procedures.

59
00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.840
Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links,

60
00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.760
and official voter guide.

61
00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.540
Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice.
